# Vulnerability details

## Impact

The main purpose of the FeeWrapper contract is to wrap a call to an arbitrary target contract that potentially includes an ETH or ERC20 payment. A fee portion of that payment is routed to a fee recipient while the rest of it is forward to the wrapped call itself.

In the case of an ERC20 payment, the total amount of the payment is first moved from the caller to the FeeWrapper contract. Then the fee amount is transferred to the fee recipient, and the rest is approved to the target contract so it can call pull the payment. This can be seen in the `_chargeFee` function:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-rubicon/blob/main/contracts/utilities/FeeWrapper.sol#L93-L106

```solidity
093:     function _chargeFee(FeeParams memory _feeParams, address _target) internal {
094:         address _feeToken = _feeParams.feeToken;
095:         uint256 _totalAmount = _feeParams.totalAmount;
096:         uint256 _feeAmount = _feeParams.feeAmount;
097:         address _feeTo = _feeParams.feeTo;
098: 
099:         // transfer total amount to the FeeWrapper
100:         IERC20(_feeToken).transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), _totalAmount);
101:         // transfer fee to the 3rd party protocol
102:         IERC20(_feeToken).transfer(_feeTo, _feeAmount);
103: 
104:         // approve tokens to the `_target`
105:         IERC20(_feeToken).approve(_target, (_totalAmount - _feeAmount));
106:     }
```

The issue is raised when the target contract doesn't use the total amount of the funds, which is a common scenario. Different types of contracts run some calculations that depend on the current state when the transaction is finally executed and pull a payment amount based on that, such as AMMs where swap operations may include a slippage factor. 

