https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-rubicon/blob/511636d889742296a54392875a35e4c0c4727bb7/contracts/V2Migrator.sol#L38-L74


# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The Vfunction migrate of the 2Migrator contract does not have any mechanisms in place to handle failed or partial migrations. If the mint function call fails, 
```
require(
            CErc20Interface(bathTokenV2).mint(amountWithdrawn) == 0,
            "migrate: MINT FAILED"
        );
```
the function will revert, but the deposited tokens will be stuck in the bathTokenV2 contract, and the user will have lost the tokens they tried to migrate. 



the developpers implemented a require that test test this case 
```
 require(
            IERC20(bathTokenV2).balanceOf(address(this)) == 0,
            "migrate: BATH TOKENS V2 STUCK IN THE CONTRACT"
        );
```
but that require will not be responsible to returning transfered funds to the caller

## Recommended Mitigation Steps
Consider implementing a mechanism to handle failed or partial migrations, you can modify the code to use a try/catch block and handle the exception by transferring the deposited tokens back to the user.

```
 try CErc20Interface(bathTokenV2).mint(amountWithdrawn) returns (uint256 error) {
        require(error == 0, "migrate: MINT FAILED");
        // ...
    } catch {
        // If mint fails, transfer deposited tokens back to the user
        SafeERC20.safeTransfer(
            bathTokenV1,
            msg.sender,
            bathTokenV1.balanceOf(address(this))
        );
        emit MigrateFailed(msg.sender, address(bathTokenV1), amountWithdrawn);
        return;
    }
```

