# Vulnerability details

## Impact
[`_borrowLimit()`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-rubicon/blob/511636d889742296a54392875a35e4c0c4727bb7/contracts/utilities/poolsUtility/Position.sol#L526-L583) is assumed to return `limit` variable, which is the number of iteration (lend=>borrow=>swap) needs to be performed to reach leverage.

It assumes that multiplying the `_assetAmount` with `_collateralFactor` will give us the amount we can borrow against `_assetAmount`
```
        while (_assetAmount <= _desiredAmount) {
            if (_limit == 0) {
		// if collateral already provided
                if (_minted != 0) {
                    uint256 _max = _maxBorrow(_bathToken);

		    // take into account previous collateral
                    _loopBorrowed = wmul(_assetAmount, _collateralFactor).add(
                        _max
                    );
                } else {
                    _loopBorrowed = wmul(_assetAmount, _collateralFactor);
                }
            } else {
                _loopBorrowed = wmul(_loopBorrowed, _collateralFactor);
            }
```
This is correct, but the thing is compound's comptroller(bathHouseV2 here) deals everything in USD price
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-04-rubicon/blob/511636d889742296a54392875a35e4c0c4727bb7/contracts/compound-v2-fork/Comptroller.sol#L896-L987
Here, on line 927, it fetches the USD price of the underlying and then multiplies it with collateralFactor. Which is different than how it is calculated in position.sol

In position.sol => borrowed = amout * collateralFactor
In comptroller => borrowed = amount * USDPrice * collateralFactor

So, the number of iterations would be wrong, which leads to an incorrect `lend=>borrow=>swap` loop, which may result in unexpected outcomes (i.e user may get liquidated)


