# Vulnerability details

## Impact
It's a common feature for some of the most popular tokens to have a blocklisting functionality. Tokens such as USDC and USDT can block transfers from and to specific addresses. This functionality is not rare and we have plenty of examples of hacker's address blocked by the owners of these tokens.
An offer can be created by anyone at rubicon, if the owner's offer (or recipient if this is a different address than the owner) is later blocklisted by the bought token (the token that the offer's owner will receive in exchange for the offered token) then every time a buyer is matched with that specific offered, the transaction will be reverted.
There is no functionality that protocol could use for remove this kind of offers, blocking part of the procotol functionality.

