# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Accounting logic in BathBuddy.sol used to determine user rewards contains a critical flaw which gives users a disproportionately high first reward which fails to account for the amount of time the position has been open.

This happens because the reward logic keeps track of an ever increasing `rewardPerToken` storage variable which is used to determine a users reward amount by retrieving a delta between `rewardPerToken` and `userRewardsPerTokenPaid`. 

```
// BathBuddy.sol:L139-155
function earned(
	address account,
	address token
) public view override returns (uint256) {
	require(friendshipStarted, "I have not started a bathToken friendship");

	return
		IERC20(myBathTokenBuddy) // Care with this?
			.balanceOf(account)
			.mul(
				rewardPerToken(token).sub(
					userRewardsPerTokenPaid[token][account]
				)
			)
			.div(1e18)
			.add(tokenRewards[token][account]);
}
```

The problem with this logic is that `userRewardsPerTokenPaid` is initially 0 and doesn't get set until after retrieving the users first reward amount.

```
// BathBuddy:L247-257
modifier updateReward(address account, address token) {
	rewardsPerTokensStored[token] = rewardPerToken(token);
	lastUpdateTime[token] = lastTimeRewardApplicable(token);
	if (account != address(0)) {
		// @admin: Sets user rewards to claim before setting userRewardsPerTokenPaid
		tokenRewards[token][account] = earned(account, token);
		userRewardsPerTokenPaid[token][account] = rewardsPerTokensStored[
			token
		];
	}
	_;
}
```

As a result, users initial reward amount will not take into account the amount of time they've been in the pool and will instead be their share of the total pool tokens. 

