# Vulnerability details

## Impact
The contract has some batch functionality which can be used to execute multiple offer executions. Both the code blocks linked above use the same logic in the end, where they call the `offer()` function. The function is however called in a wrong manner, using `this.offer()` as shown below.
```solidity
this.offer(
  payAmts[i],
  ERC20(payGems[i]),
  buyAmts[i],
  ERC20(buyGems[i])
  );
```

The issue here is that when calling functions with the `this` keyword, the `msg.sender` is not preserved. Thus when the `offer()` function is eventually called, the contract sees the `msg.sender` is the rubicon contract itself, not the actual user. Thus the contract does incorrect executions which can lead to loss of funds.

In the `offer()` function, we see that the contract draws out the required tokens from `msg.sender`.
```solidity
require(pay_gem.transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), pay_amt));
```
Since here the contract itself is the `msg.sender`, it draws out the balance from itself to create offers. If a user submits a very badly priced offer through the batch functions, the user can then buy the tokens very cheap since the contract essentially funds the badly priced offers. Thus users can drain the contract of funds using bad offers.

