# Vulnerability details

## Impact

When a user sends both ETH and tokens to `rubricall()`, their tokens are lost/not properly accounted for. `rubricall()` is a function used for handling both ETH and ERC20 tokens, so it is a reasonable user assumption that they expect that sending ERC20 and ETH to work. Additionally, we are not sure how FeeWrapper low level calls will be used by external contracts.

## Proof of Concept

The rubicall function is designed to go down the _rubicall logic path when the msg.sender intends to send tokens, and the _rubicallPayable path when user deposits ETH. 

```
 function rubicall(
        CallParams memory params
    ) external payable returns (bytes memory) {
        if (msg.value == 0) {
            return _rubicall(params);
        } else {
            return _rubicallPayable(params);
        }
    }
```

However, when a user deposits both ETH and tokens are sent, then `_rubicall` is not called, as it does not pass the `(fails the msg.value==0)` check in the if statement. This means that the accounting for the tokens that the user sent is not done, and hence their tokens are lost. The ETH portion of the function is still processed correctly.

## Tools Used

Manual Review

## Recommended Mitigation Steps

Set that if `msg.value != 0` and `(params)` is not empty, then execute both `_rubicall` and `rubicallPayable`

