# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Users will not be able to open a position for a token that does not have 18 decimals.

## Proof of Concept
Whenever a user opens a position and wants to use leverage, they will borrow from a lender. To be able to lend more assets for leverage, there is a check for the maximum borrow amount. Unfortunately, in that check, there is an assumption that the underlying token has 18 decimals, which is not always the case according to the documentation. The protocol aims to support other tokens as well.
>We intend to only add blue-chip assets (e.g. WETH, WBTC, USDC, USDT, DAI)

The problem that oracle`s `getUnderlyingPrice` works like this.
>RETURNS: The price of the asset in USD as an unsigned integer scaled up by 10 ^ (36 - underlying asset decimals). E.g. WBTC has 8 decimal places, so the return value is scaled up by 1e28.

This means that `_max` will be 0 for some cases when decimals are less than 18, causing the `require` statement to fail. On the other hand, users will not be able to borrow at all if the token's decimal is greater than 18 because the computed amount for maximum borrow will be too high for lending.

